1
00:00:00,900 --> 00:00:06,715
The brush uncovers a jar with two handles and a pointed base — a shape made for stacking in a ship's hold,

2
00:00:06,715 --> 00:00:09,189
and a shape nobody in India was making.

3
00:00:13,900 --> 00:00:19,530
It is an amphora, from the Mediterranean, and it was found in India because somebody sailed it here.

4
00:00:23,500 --> 00:00:28,716
The best single source is a merchant's handbook: the Periplus of the Erythraean Sea,

5
00:00:28,716 --> 00:00:33,626
written in Greek in the first century CE by someone who clearly knew the route.

6
00:00:33,626 --> 00:00:35,160
Excavation at the ports.

7
00:00:35,160 --> 00:00:35,958
Coin hoards.

8
00:00:35,958 --> 00:00:39,572
And Tamil poetry, which describes the ships arriving.

9
00:00:44,800 --> 00:00:47,936
The thing that makes this trade possible is the monsoon.

10
00:00:47,936 --> 00:00:52,832
Sail along the coast and the voyage from Egypt to India takes the better part of a year.

11
00:00:52,832 --> 00:01:00,173
Use the south-west monsoon to run straight across the open sea, and the north-east monsoon to come back, and it takes a season.

12
00:01:04,233 --> 00:01:08,117
Classical tradition credits a sailor named Hippalus with working this out,

13
00:01:08,117 --> 00:01:12,975
though the knowledge was almost certainly already in use among Arab and Indian sailors.

14
00:01:16,467 --> 00:01:18,370
So the ports become rich.

15
00:01:18,370 --> 00:01:24,302
On the west coast, Barygaza — modern Bharuch — and Muziris in the Chera country.

16
00:01:24,302 --> 00:01:29,874
On the east, Arikamedu, Puhar and, further north, Tamralipti in Bengal.

17
00:01:32,200 --> 00:01:39,235
The Periplus names many of these and lists what each one bought and sold, which is the kind of detail historians almost never get.

18
00:01:43,767 --> 00:01:44,545
What moved?

19
00:01:44,545 --> 00:01:50,187
Out of India: pepper above all, which the Roman world wanted in quantity; also pearls,

20
00:01:50,187 --> 00:01:55,740
ivory, fine cotton, silk passing through from China, precious stones and spices.

21
00:02:00,400 --> 00:02:06,275
Into India: gold and silver coin, wine, olive oil, glass, fine pottery, and coral.

22
00:02:06,275 --> 00:02:11,954
Notice the asymmetry — India sent goods and received a great deal of money.

23
00:02:16,767 --> 00:02:18,622
The money shows up in the ground.

24
00:02:18,622 --> 00:02:26,177
Hoards of Roman coins have been found across peninsular India, concentrated in the south — which is also where the pepper came from.

25
00:02:28,500 --> 00:02:35,609
Roman writers complained about it: Pliny the Elder claimed the eastern trade was draining the empire of its currency every year.

26
00:02:35,609 --> 00:02:41,089
That complaint is quoted in nearly every textbook, and it is worth asking how reliable it is.

27
00:02:46,333 --> 00:02:48,163
Did the trade really drain Rome?

28
00:02:48,163 --> 00:02:54,340
One reading takes Pliny more or less at his word and points to the sheer quantity of coin found in India.

29
00:02:58,900 --> 00:03:04,320
The other treats his figure as moralising rhetoric about luxury rather than an economic estimate,

30
00:03:04,320 --> 00:03:09,574
notes that coins found in India may have circulated as bullion by weight rather than as money,

31
00:03:09,574 --> 00:03:12,339
and points out that Rome exported real goods too.

32
00:03:12,339 --> 00:03:18,280
Most historians now sit between the two: the flow of coin was substantial, the drain was exaggerated.

33
00:03:23,833 --> 00:03:26,270
Here is the memory tip, carved in stone.

34
00:03:26,270 --> 00:03:31,681
Pepper out, gold in — and the monsoon is what made it quick enough to be worth doing.

35
00:03:38,533 --> 00:03:39,160
Dig Quiz.

36
00:03:39,160 --> 00:03:40,349
Pause and name it.

37
00:03:40,349 --> 00:03:47,475
First: the first-century Greek merchant's handbook describing the ports and goods of the Indian Ocean trade.

38
00:03:52,267 --> 00:03:54,587
The Periplus of the Erythraean Sea.

39
00:03:54,587 --> 00:04:00,837
Second: the seasonal wind system that let ships cross the open sea instead of hugging the coast.

40
00:04:00,837 --> 00:04:01,975
The monsoon.

41
00:04:04,833 --> 00:04:08,221
Third: the great port of the Ganga delta in Bengal.

42
00:04:08,221 --> 00:04:09,003
Tamralipti.

43
00:04:09,003 --> 00:04:15,818
In Mains, a frequent question asks what Indo-Roman trade tells us about the ancient Indian economy.

44
00:04:19,533 --> 00:04:27,357
Use the ports, the goods, the coin hoards, the craft production behind the exports — and be careful with the drain-of-gold claim.

45
00:04:31,033 --> 00:04:34,837
An amphora takes its place in the gallery, and the fifth room is full:

46
00:04:34,837 --> 00:04:38,834
a jar that was carried thousands of miles and then thrown away here.

47
00:04:42,200 --> 00:04:44,837
Next gallery: the north is unified again,

48
00:04:44,837 --> 00:04:51,115
and a period begins that later writers would call golden — with some reason, and some exaggeration.

49
00:04:51,115 --> 00:04:54,993
To catch it when it's out, subscribe to Gyaan-X Studios.

